==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|chastize||archaic in British English|rare in American English}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂eǵ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|chastisen}}, from {{der|en|fro|chastier}}, from {{der|en|la|castīgō}}. See also the doublets {{doublet|en|chasten|castigate|notext=1}} and cf. also {{l|en|chaste}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/tʃæˈstaɪz/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-chastise.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈt͡ʃæstaɪz/|/t͡ʃæˈstaɪz/|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪz|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[punish]], especially by [[corporal punishment]].
#* {{RQ:King James Version|1 Kings|12|11|passage=And now whereas my father did lade you with a heauy yoke, I wil adde to your yoke: my father hath '''chastised''' you with whippes, but I will '''chastise''' you with scorpions.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2 May 1885|author=w:John Thomas Caine|editor=John Irvine|title=“Mormon” Protest Against Injustice: An Appeal for Constitutional and Religious Liberty|year_published=1885|page=12|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=CXVNAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA12|text=An army was sent to '''chastise''' an unoffending people; to subdue an imaginary insurrection.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1958|author=w:Elizabeth Colson|title=Marriage and the Family among the Plateau Tonga of Northern Rhodesia|page=142|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=umq7AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA142|text=Thus only the husband is in a position to '''chastise''' her, for his own relatives may not exert any physical force over her.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|author=William P. Loewe|title=The College Student’s Introduction to Christology|isbn=081465018X|page=46|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=o7e1PCoH1dIC&pg=PA46|text=If even Israel, the people chosen to be God’s own, scorned and neglected the values God had revealed to be what God willed and desired, then God would marshal the forces of history to '''chastise''' them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018|author=Nyx Smith|title=Shadowrun Legends: Striper Assassin|publisher=Catalyst Game Labs
|passage=“There is a man who must be '''chastised''',” she says softly, lightly. “'''Chastised''' in a physical way. It should not be difficult. Not for you.”}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[castigate]]; to [[scold]] or [[censure]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:criticize}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1975|author=Cornelius Osgood|title=The Chinese: A Study of a Hong Kong Community|url=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=NUEsAAAAMAAJ|volume=2|isbn=0816504180|page=485|text=She feels definitely that Lung Shing is her town, and is not hesitant to '''chastise''' people who she thinks are not behaving properly—such as, for example, a woman using loud, vulgar language on the street—although in so doing she may only turn the direction of abuse on herself.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1987|month=September|author=w:Eric Foner|title=The Second American Revolution|chapter2=The Second American Revolution|editor2=w:Bertell Ollman; Jonathan Birnbaum|title2=The United States Constitution: 200 Years of {{...}} Socialist Criticism|year2=1990|isbn2=0814761690|page2=202|pageurl2=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=hQ4UCgAAQBAJ&pg=PA202|text=Only last year Attorney General Edwin Meese '''chastised''' the Supreme Court for a series of decisions based on the legal doctrine of “indoctrination”—that is, that the 14th Amendment requires the states to respect the prohibitions on abuse of power that the Bill of Rights had originally applied to the federal government.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=Douglas E. Foley|title=The Heartland Chronicles|isbn=0812233263|page=viii|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=cZh0BAAAQBAJ&pg=PP8|text=My urban, academic friends '''chastise''' me for romanticizing rural life.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=23 August 2021|author=w:Rebecca Solnit|title=Big oil coined ‘carbon footprints’ to blame us for their greed. Keep them on the hook|titleurl=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/aug/23/big-oil-coined-carbon-footprints-to-blame-us-for-their-greed-keep-them-on-the-hook|journal=w:The Guardian|text=People pop up all the time to boast of their domestic arrangements or '''chastise''' others for what they eat or how they get around.}}

